<p>North Korea’s Naegohyang FC women’s football club arrived at Incheon International Airport on May 17 to compete in the AFC Women’s Champions League, the first visit by North Korean athletes to South Korea in about eight years. Reports put the delegation at 27 players and 12 staff. The club is scheduled to play South Korea’s Suwon FC Women in a semifinal in Suwon on Wednesday, May 20. South Korea’s Unification Ministry approved the visit under the inter-Korean exchange law, with permission for the team to stay through next weekend, although the club could leave earlier if eliminated. The trip follows North Korea’s statements calling the South its “most hostile state” and rejecting reunification, while South Korean President Lee Jae Myung has called for improved ties.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/ElpVdPssae5Xl1oyreHlxm0WAWpOb9oD9Wbwjq2vUEVZJHFQHFakn2KS122JE51VPZiYPdiCvM_R5Mk4Cgpa1KNqgzyd2Ub7T2Z4J2ublxJFMCnPLl8g0sCXf0TWTiY-ew79nUDL8fYw_FWTDGz0aDH0aQYLCbV7Joa5NJ7RC3A2CrBkpLqnN0W4jGiLdgYZ4YHkU8g3NWugk7NQvgncUA' alt='Members of Naegohyang, a North Korean women’s soccer team, arriving at Incheon International Airport in South Korea on Sunday.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Airport arrival: The team arrived from Beijing, walked past reporters without answering questions and left the airport under police escort.</li><li>Ticket demand: Yonhap, citing South Korea’s football federation, said all 7,087 tickets made available to the general public sold out within a day.</li><li>Venue detail: The semifinal is scheduled at Suwon Sports Complex, according to Yonhap News TV.</li><li>Team background: Naegohyang is based in Pyongyang and was founded in 2012, according to Yonhap News TV.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>New York Times: The trip alone is unlikely to produce a diplomatic thaw, although past sports events have sometimes helped create openings between the Koreas. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/05/17/world/asia/north-korea-naegohyang-soccer-football.html'>New York Times</a>)</li><li>South Korean civic groups: Civic groups greeted the team at Incheon International Airport and organized a joint cheering squad to support both teams at the Suwon match. (<a href='https://www.yonhapnewstv.co.kr/news/AKR20260517151247VEc'>Yonhap News TV</a>, <a href='https://www.yonhapnewstv.co.kr/news/MYH20260517150505yxD'>Yonhap News TV</a>)</li><li>Khan: The newspaper said the delegation presented passports rather than South Korea-issued visit certificates, a detail it tied to Pyongyang’s emphasis on the trip as consistent with North Korea’s two-state framing. (<a href='https://www.khan.co.kr/article/202605172115025/'>Khan</a>)</li><li>Cheering squad official: A South Korean cheering squad official said the visit showed the possibility of inter-Korean cooperation in multilateral arenas. (<a href='https://en.yna.co.kr/view/AEN20260517000300315'>Yonhap News Agency</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/05/17/world/asia/north-korea-naegohyang-soccer-football.html'>A North Korean Soccer Team Makes a Rare Visit to South Korea</a> - nytimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/asia/east-asia/article/3353876/north-korean-footballers-arrive-south-match-tickets-sell-out'>North Korean footballers arrive in South, match tickets sell out</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dw.com/en/north-korean-women-s-football-team-makes-rare-visit-to-south/a-77186062'>North Korean women&#x27;s football team makes rare visit to South</a> - dw.com</li><li><a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/north-korean-womens-soccer-team-arrives-south-korea-133045454'>North Korean women&#x27;s soccer team arrives in South Korea for regional tournament</a> - abcnews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/may/17/afc-women-champions-league-north-korea-south-korea-naegohyang-fc-suwon'>‘Tearing down barriers’: North Korean footballers arrive in Seoul for first time in eight years